{
  "ID": "11.1",
  "Context/Background": "Tumor-infiltrating B cells play a significant role in tumor development, progression, and prognosis, yet a comprehensive classification system is lacking. To address this gap, we present a pan-cancer single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) atlas of tumor-infiltrating B and plasma cells across a large sample cohort. \n\nWe isolated B cells and plasma cells from 15 studies spanning 7 cancer types as data sources. After applying batch correction and integrating the data, we removed low-quality cells and determined the optimal number of clusters. Using standard Seurat methods for integration, clustering, and differential expression analysis, we confirmed the absence of batch effects, with no study- or cancer type-specific clusters. \n\nWe employed a graph-based clustering method to identify TIL-Bs with recurring phenotypes, uncovering a total of 10 clusters. Step one of cluster annotation used canonical markers like CD19 and MS4A1 (CD20), which are prevalent throughout B cell development but decline in plasma cells. Markers such as CD38, JCHAIN, and SDC1 (CD138) are prominent in plasma cells, but less so in earlier stages. This revealed four B cell clusters, two intermediate clusters with proliferative and germinal center (GC) features, and four plasma cell clusters. ",
  "Prompt": "Based on upregulated pathways and differentially expressed markers in each cluster, assign functional phenotypes to each of the 4 B-cell clusters (clusters 1-4).  Provide a discussion of the reasoning behind cluster assignments, highlighting important genes and pathways where relevant. ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-15 21:06:37",
  "elapsed_seconds": 243.47,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Cluster 1 – Resting/Follicular (Naïve-like) B cells  \nMarkers & pathways: High expression of TCL1A, FCER2 (CD23), GPR183 (EBI2), FOXP1 and multiple HLA-II genes (HLA-DP, ‑DQ, ‑DR).  This constellation (TCL1A/FOXP1 for survival, FCER2 for low‐affinity IgE receptor, GPR183 for follicular positioning and robust antigen‐presentation via HLA-II) is characteristic of resting, antigen‐inexperienced (naïve) or early follicular B cells poised for germinal-center entry.  \n\nCluster 2 – Innate-like/Marginal Zone-like B cells  \nMarkers & pathways: Modest upregulation of LY86 (MD-1), BLK (B-lymphoid tyrosine kinase) and LTB (lymphotoxin-β).  BLK and LY86 support tonic BCR signaling typical of innate-like B cells (e.g., marginal-zone or B1 subsets), while LTB drives lymphoid‐organ organization and tertiary‐lymphoid structure development.  This suggests a structural/support role rather than active antigen response.  \n\nCluster 3 – Interferon-Activated Antigen-Presenting B cells  \nMarkers & pathways: Very high ISG signature (ISG15, IFI44L, IFITM1, OAS1), plus upregulated CD86 and HLA-DP/-DQ/-DR family.  The co-occurrence of interferon‐stimulated genes and costimulatory/antigen‐presentation machinery defines an IFN‐activated, APC‐competent B cell subset—likely recruited to inflamed tumor sites to present antigen to T cells.  \n\nCluster 4 – Activated/Germinal-Center-Homing B cells  \nMarkers & pathways: Strong upregulation of CD69 (early activation), CD83 (GC‐entry marker), CCR7 and CXCR5 (homing receptors for T‐ and B‐cell zones), REL/NFKBID (NF-κB modulators), and CD40‐pathway components.  This profile fits activated, GC‐seeking B cells or nascent memory B cells undergoing clonal expansion under T-cell help.  \n\nDiscussion  \nBy combining avg_log2FC marker patterns with known B-cell biology, we see a continuum from Cluster 1’s resting/naïve state (high TCL1A, GPR183, HLA-II) through Cluster 2’s innate‐like niche (LY86, BLK, LTB) into two activated phenotypes: Cluster 3 as interferon‐driven APCs (ISGs + CD86/HLA) and Cluster 4 as NF-κB–dependent, GC-homing activated cells (CD69, CCR7, CXCR5, CD83).  These assignments align with emerging models of intratumoral B-cell heterogeneity, where both innate‐like and highly activated, antigen‐presenting subsets co‐exist alongside bona fide GC‐phase B cells.",
